WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.140
Proposition three is about whether existing higher income-tax rates

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:07.720
should expire.

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.720
Voters approved these rates in twenty twelve and extended

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.940
them in twenty sixteen.

5
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.960
They are now scheduled to end after twenty thirty.

6
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.720
The additional rates apply to roughly the highest-earning two

7
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.580
percent of taxpayers.

8
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.200
This is a tax on income, such as wages

9
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.760
or investment profits, rather than a tax on everything

10
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.740
someone owns.

11
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.640
Proposition three would make those higher rates permanent.

12
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.880
It would keep the current system beyond its scheduled

13
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.060
expiration, rather than introducing a new increase in everybody’s

14
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:41.700
rate today.

15
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.800
Imagine a teacher planning future classes and support services.

16
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.520
If an existing revenue stream ends, a budget planner

17
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.260
must consider cuts, replacement taxes, or other funding.

18
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.420
Keeping that revenue can avoid that particular gap, but

19
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.740
does not promise any school a specific staffing level.

20
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.960
School budgets depend on the wider state budget.

21
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.060
The analyst estimates five to fifteen billion dollars of

22
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.320
annual revenue maintained after the scheduled expiration.

23
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.240
The wide range reflects the volatile incomes and investment

24
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.820
gains of high earners.

25
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.600
It is not a guaranteed fifteen billion every year.

26
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.100
Roughly forty percent would support schools and community colleges.

27
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.720
The remainder supports other state programs and reserves.

28
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.780
Those shares describe the analyst’s estimate, rather than a

29
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.820
promise that every dollar goes to classroom instruction.

30
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.240
A no vote does not lower rates immediately.

31
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.260
The scheduled expiration would still occur in twenty thirty-one.

32
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.080
Comparing yes and no therefore means comparing future budgets,

33
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.400
not just this year’s tax bill.

34
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.240
A voter might prefer protecting existing revenue for education

35
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.600
and other services, with the additional tax concentrated on

36
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.260
high earners.

37
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.740
Making the rates permanent removes repeated expiration deadlines, though

38
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.280
it does not make the revenue itself predictable.

39
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.420
You might ask whether a temporary tax should stay

40
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:09.760
permanent.

41
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.280
A skeptical voter could want the state to honor

42
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.240
the sunset or reduce reliance on a small, volatile

43
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.220
tax base.

44
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.780
They may worry about effects on investment or where

45
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.300
high earners live.

46
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.080
The size of behavioral effects is uncertain.

47
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.340
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 58

48
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.940
percent said yes, 40 percent no, and 2 percent

49
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:33.500
were unsure.

50
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.460
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

51
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.540
A later September Berkeley survey found 54 percent yes,

52
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.980
34 percent no, and 13 percent undecided.

53
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.400
Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does

54
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.620
not by itself establish a trend.

55
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.460
Yes makes the current higher rates permanent; no preserves

56
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.560
their scheduled expiration.

57
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.600
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

58
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.220
and official voter guide.

59
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.100
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
